---@field delivery_rate number|distilled.Null Emails this provider accepted, divided by emails sent to it (0-1). Acceptance is not inbox placement: a provider can accept a message and still file it as spam. Null when the underlying metric could not be loaded from AWS, which is not the same as zero.
---@field bounce_rate number|distilled.Null Hard (permanent) bounces at this provider, divided by emails sent to it (0-1). Null when the underlying metric could not be loaded from AWS.
---@field transient_bounce_rate number|distilled.Null Soft (transient) bounces at this provider, divided by emails sent to it (0-1). These are deferrals the provider may accept on a retry, such as a full mailbox, greylisting or rate limiting, so they are counted apart from permanent bounces. Null when the underlying metric could not be loaded from AWS.
---@field complaint_rate number|distilled.Null Spam complaints from this provider, divided by the deliveries it reports complaints for (0-1). Null when there is no rate to state — the provider runs no feedback loop, or nothing was delivered — and also when the metric could not be loaded from AWS.
---@field complaint_base number Deliveries the provider reports complaints for, which is what `complaint_rate` divides by. Far smaller than `emails_sent`, so a caller deciding whether the rate rests on enough volume has to weigh it against this. Zero when there is no base.
---@field unavailable posthog.hog_flows.IspSendingHealthUnavailableList Rates AWS did not return for this provider, from `delivery`, `bounce`, `transient_bounce` and `complaint`. A rate named here is missing, not zero, and the UI says so rather than showing a number.

