---Whether PostHog paused this one workflow's email sending, and why.
---@class posthog.hog_flows.WorkflowEmailPauseStatus
---@field email_sending_paused boolean True while this workflow's email is paused because its spam complaint or hard bounce rate crossed a threshold. Other workflows in the project keep sending.
---@field email_sending_paused_at string|distilled.Null When the pause started; null when not paused.
---@field email_sending_paused_reason string Plain-language reason for the pause, naming the signal and the window. Empty when not paused.
---@field email_sending_resumed_at string|distilled.Null When sending was last resumed. Detector windows start after this, so resuming does not immediately re-trip on older feedback. Null if never paused.

---Bounce/complaint rates over the last 30 days of workflow email, computed on the fly from app metrics.
---@class posthog.hog_flows.WorkflowEmailSendingRates
---@field bounce_rate number Hard (permanent) bounces / emails sent over the last 30 days (0-1), matching how AWS counts its bounce rate — transient bounces (greylisting, mailbox full) are excluded. Bounces are counted when the feedback arrives, so the ratio is approximate at the window boundary and capped at 1.
---@field complaint_rate number Spam complaints / emails sent over the last 30 days (0-1). Complaints are counted when the feedback arrives, so the ratio is approximate at the window boundary and capped at 1.
---@field emails_sent number Emails sent in the last 30 days.
---@field hog_flow_id string The workflow these rates are for.
---@field hog_flow_name string Display name of the workflow; empty for unnamed workflows.
---@field email_sending_paused boolean True when PostHog paused this workflow's email automatically because its complaint or hard bounce rate crossed a threshold. Independent of the AWS tenant verdict and of the project-wide suspension.
---@field email_sending_paused_at string|distilled.Null When the pause started; null when not paused.
---@field email_sending_paused_reason string Plain-language reason for the pause, naming the signal and the window. Empty when not paused.

---@class posthog.hog_flows.WorkflowProposal
---@field id string
---@field title string Short summary of the proposed change.
---@field rationale string Why the producer thinks this change is worth making.
---@field content posthog.hog_flows.WorkflowProposalContentMap Only the content fields the proposal changes. Valid keys: actions, edges, trigger_masking, conversion, exit_condition, email_sending_rate_limit, variables. Each value has the same shape as on the workflow itself.
---@field evidence posthog.hog_flows.WorkflowProposalEvidenceMap The numbers behind the proposal, read back by name. Five keys are required: `metric`, the metric name; `current_value`, its value as a number (a rate as a fraction, 0.0865, never a string); `unit`, either `rate` or `count`, since 1.0 is either every message or one of them; `n`, the denominator that value was computed over; and `guardrails`, a list of {metric, value, n, unit} counter-metrics read over the same window, empty only if none apply. PostHog then reads the step's own metrics at `base_version` when the suggestion is filed and stores them under `measured`; the page shows that reading and flags a disagreement with yours. Also conventional: target_value, window (a relative one such as `-7d`, or an ISO 8601 interval `start/end` with offsets, which PostHog reads back exactly), query, app_source_id. A rate with no denominator lets a reviewer mistake noise for a result, a target with no counter-metrics hides a change that lifts one number by harming another, and a number under a key of your own reads to a person as no evidence at all.
---@field step_id string|distilled.Null The workflow step this is about. Set for a change to one step: the evidence and the outcome then read that step's metrics, so a change to one email in a sequence is not measured against the rest. Null only for a change that spans the workflow, such as its exit condition or a step being taken out, which is measured on the workflow's own numbers.
---@field base_version number Live workflow version this was authored against. Approving compares the steps and fields this changes against that version to tell whether somebody else already changed them.
---@field is_stale boolean Whether approving this would undo an edit made since it was proposed. False while the workflow only changed elsewhere, because approving merges only what the proposal changes.
---@field status posthog.hog_flows.WorkflowProposalStatusEnum
---@field source_id string|distilled.Null Stable id of the producing agent run or finding, e.g. 'run:<run id>:finding:<finding id>'.
---@field created_at string
---@field resolved_at string|distilled.Null
---@field resolved_by posthog.hog_flows.UserBasic|distilled.Null
---@field applied_version number|distilled.Null Workflow version the approved change went live as.
---@field rejection_reason string Why the person who rejected this suggestion rejected it, or empty when they gave no reason.

---Only the content fields the proposal changes. Valid keys: actions, edges, trigger_masking, conversion, exit_condition, email_sending_rate_limit, variables. Each value has the same shape as on the workflow itself.
---@alias posthog.hog_flows.WorkflowProposalContentMap { [string]: any }

---The numbers behind the proposal, read back by name. Five keys are required: `metric`, the metric name; `current_value`, its value as a number (a rate as a fraction, 0.0865, never a string); `unit`, either `rate` or `count`, since 1.0 is either every message or one of them; `n`, the denominator that value was computed over; and `guardrails`, a list of {metric, value, n, unit} counter-metrics read over the same window, empty only if none apply. PostHog then reads the step's own metrics at `base_version` when the suggestion is filed and stores them under `measured`; the page shows that reading and flags a disagreement with yours. Also conventional: target_value, window (a relative one such as `-7d`, or an ISO 8601 interval `start/end` with offsets, which PostHog reads back exactly), query, app_source_id. A rate with no denominator lets a reviewer mistake noise for a result, a target with no counter-metrics hides a change that lifts one number by harming another, and a number under a key of your own reads to a person as no evidence at all.
---@alias posthog.hog_flows.WorkflowProposalEvidenceMap { [string]: any }

---@class posthog.hog_flows.WorkflowProposalMetric
---@field metric string What was measured, e.g. 'email open rate'.
---@field value number|distilled.Null The rate over the window, or null when there was nothing to divide.
---@field n number Observations the rate was computed over.
